cff-version: 1.2.0
message: Please cite the paper; this repository supplies its source and finite checks.
type: software
title: "A trace costs one quantifier: the exact complexity of perfect play in nonlocal games"
version: 1.0.0
date-released: '2026-11-04'
license: [CC-BY-4.0, MIT, Apache-2.0]
authors: [{"family-names": "Douglas", "given-names": "Seth", "orcid": "https://orcid.org/0009-0007-4708-3252"}, {"family-names": "Mghirbi", "given-names": "Nidhal", "orcid": "https://orcid.org/0009-0005-6534-1118"}]
keywords: ["nonlocal games", "arithmetical hierarchy", "computability", "von Neumann algebras", "zero-error communication", "quantum information"]
abstract: "Given a finite nonlocal game, can the players win with certainty? We find the exact level of the arithmetical hierarchy at which this is decided, for each standard model of quantum strategy. With normal states on tensor products of Hilbert spaces, perfect play is $\\Sigma^0_2$-complete, also when the strategy must have finite entanglement entropy, so it cannot be decided even with an oracle for the halting problem. This completes a table in which finite-dimensional perfection is $\\Sigma^0_1$-complete, commuting perfection is $\\Pi^0_1$-complete and value one is $\\Pi^0_2$-complete. Inside the commuting model the answer depends on the von Neumann type of the players' algebra: perfect play in a type $\\mathrm{II}_1$ algebra, or in any semifinite algebra, is $\\Sigma^0_2$-complete, while in a type $\\mathrm{III}_\\lambda$ factor, for each fixed $\\lambda\\in[0,1]$, it is equivalent to commuting perfection. In this sense a trace costs one existential quantifier. Commuting perfect play without semifinite perfect play is $\\Pi^0_2$-complete; the remaining semifinite-without-spatial differences are $D_2(\\Sigma^0_2)$-complete. In the hardness proofs the existential witness is a finite object that every perfect strategy must choose: the order of a clock phase in the spatial model, or the depth of a critical binary tree in semifinite algebras. The same level governs communication: deciding whether a finite classical channel can send a given number of messages in one use with zero error, helped by normal entanglement, is $\\Sigma^0_2$-complete, and some channel's zero-error codes all need infinite entanglement entropy. Positive computable moment orders below one form a strict $\\Sigma^0_2$-complete resource ladder; the infimal critical indices of spatially perfect games (attained by the realizing games) are exactly the right-c.e.-in-$0'$ reals in $[0,1]$.\n\nEarlier versions of Theorems A, B and C, of Theorems 3.1 and 8.1, of the trace-class correspondence, of the summability threshold, of the commuting row and of the I-only and infinite-entropy-only classifications appeared in our preprints Perfect play with spatial quantum strategies is Sigma^0_2-complete (doi:10.5281/zenodo.23910986), Perfect play has different arithmetical complexity in semifinite and type III algebras (doi:10.5281/zenodo.23925091), and One-shot zero-error communication with normal entanglement is Sigma^0_2-complete (doi:10.5281/zenodo.23924868), which this paper consolidates and supersedes. Apart from those preprints we know of no prior statement of these results; Theorem D and the completion of the semifinite-but-not-spatial cells are new here. The Π⁰₁-completeness of commuting perfection is known from Slofstra, Tsirelson's problem and an embedding theorem for groups arising from non-local games, JAMS 33 (2020), doi:10.1090/jams/929, and Mousavi-Nezhadi-Yuen, Nonlocal Games, Compression Theorems, and the Arithmetical Hierarchy, arXiv:2110.04651; what is new in Theorem B is that every fixed type-III subtype has exactly that language."
